/*
Example purpose
---------------
This file is based on the usual testImpactor/constant/kinematicCloudProperties.
The important additions to notice are:

1. analyticPositionTracking in the solution dictionary

    analyticPositionTracking false;

   This is a solver-specific option.  false keeps the legacy/OpenFOAM-style
   position update, where the tracking displacement is based on the current
   parcel velocity.  true enables the newer frozen-coefficient analytic
   displacement update, which can reduce wall-hit position and hit-time errors
   for small particles with short relaxation times.

2. distance-based trajectory output in cloudFunctions

    trajectoryByDistance
    {
        type          distanceTrajectory;
        interval      1e-4;
        writeInitial  true;
        writeOnPatch  true;
    }

In the usual setup, Lagrangian cloud fields are written only when the OpenFOAM
time controls decide to write, and ParticlePostProcessing records particles
only when they hit selected patches.  That is useful for fate statistics, but it
is awkward for drawing trajectories because adjacent points are controlled by
time, not by travelled distance.

The distanceTrajectory function writes a trajectory point whenever a parcel has
moved by interval [m].  The resulting path has almost uniform spatial spacing
and is independent of controlDict.writeInterval.  Use a smaller interval for
smoother curves and a larger interval for smaller output files.
*/

solution
{
    active          true;
    coupled         false;
    transient       yes;
    cellValueSourceCorrection off;
    maxCo           0.5;

    // Solver-specific position tracking option:
    //   false: legacy/OpenFOAM-style displacement using the current velocity
    //   true : analytic frozen-coefficient displacement over each parcel step
    //
    // Keep false when comparing against the usual behaviour.  Set true when
    // small-particle wall-hit position/time accuracy is more important.
    analyticPositionTracking false;


	//Interpolation/integration schemes for the Lagrangian Particle Tracking and
    //treatment of source terms
    interpolationSchemes    {
        rho             cell;
        U               cellPoint;
        mu              cell;
		/*Available schemes are: cell, cellPatchConstrained, cellPoint
		  cellPointFace, cellPointWallModified, pointMVC                     */
    }

    integrationSchemes    {
        U               analytical;
    }

    sourceTerms  {
      schemes       {
        // U semiImplicit 1;
		// explicit or semiImplicit, relaxCoeff for each of the fields
      }
    }
}


